{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block title %}Track Record · {{ site_name }}{% endblock %}
{% block description %}The live, forward-only prediction record for {{ site_name }}, kept separate from the 2014-2025 backtest.{% endblock %}
{% block content %}

<h1>Track record</h1>
<p class="lede">
  Two separate boxes, on purpose. The backtest below is historical evidence
  that the method works in general. The live record is this specific,
  forward-published set of predictions, scored only after the fact. They are
  never averaged together into one number &mdash; doing that would let a
  strong backtest paper over a bad live run, or vice versa.
</p>

<h2>Live record</h2>
{% if first_round_id %}
<p>Recording began with the round published on {{ first_round_id }}. Every prediction in it was written to a file and committed before its matches kicked off; results are added afterwards, in a separate step, to a <code>result</code> field that starts as <code>null</code> and is filled in once &mdash; the probabilities themselves are never edited.</p>
{% else %}
<p>No predictions have been published yet.</p>
{% endif %}

<div class="card">
{% if live.n == 0 %}
<p><strong>0 matches reconciled yet.</strong> {% if first_round_id %}This round's fixtures have not been played (or their results have not yet been fetched and matched back to the prediction file). Check back after kickoff.{% endif %}</p>
{% else %}
<p>
  <strong>{{ live.n }} match{{ 'es' if live.n != 1 else '' }}</strong> resolved
  across {{ live.rounds }} round{{ 's' if live.rounds != 1 else '' }}.
  Mean RPS (calibrated): {{ '%.4f'|format(live.mean_rps_cal) }}.
</p>
<p class="notice">
  Too few matches to mean anything yet (n = {{ live.n }}, threshold n =
  {{ live_min_n }}). At this sample size the uncertainty on any summary
  statistic swamps the statistic itself. This model is not touched while
  that is true &mdash; changing it in response to early results would
  invalidate the pre-registration the rest of this site relies on.
</p>
{% endif %}
</div>

<h2>Backtest (2014&ndash;2025, reference only)</h2>
<p>This is the one confirmation run described in full on the <a href="methodology.html">Methodology</a> page. It is historical, not forward-published, and is shown here only as a reference point &mdash; never combined with the live numbers above.</p>
<div class="card card--muted">
<dl class="meta">
  <dt>Matches</dt><dd>{{ '{:,}'.format(run.n_matches) }} ({{ run.season_from }}&ndash;{{ run.season_to }})</dd>
  <dt>Verdict</dt><dd><span class="badge badge--ok">{{ run.verdict }}</span></dd>
  <dt>d_RPS (market &minus; model)</dt><dd>{{ '%.4f'|format(run.d_rps) }} [{{ '%.4f'|format(run.d_rps_ci_lo) }}, {{ '%.4f'|format(run.d_rps_ci_hi) }}]</dd>
  <dt>Gap closed</dt><dd>{{ '%.0f'|format(run.gap_closed_rps * 100) }}%</dd>
</dl>
</div>

<h2>How to verify this yourself</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Every published round is a JSON file in <code>predictions/</code>, committed to the project's git repository, with probabilities that are never edited after publication &mdash; only a <code>result</code> field is appended once a match is played.</li>
  <li>Recompute the SHA-256 of any prediction file and compare it against the value printed on that round's page.</li>
  <li>Once results exist, the numbers above are rebuilt directly from those same JSON files (not from a database or a spreadsheet) &mdash; anyone can re-run the same aggregation against the public files.</li>
  <li>Reproduce the backtest above with <code>{{ run.reproduce_cmd }}</code>.</li>
</ol>
<p>
  Independent timestamping (OpenTimestamps), Wayback Machine snapshots and a
  reproducible re-scoring script (<code>verify.py</code>) are part of this
  project's published plan and are not live yet &mdash; this page will link
  to them once they are.
</p>

{% endblock %}
